i'm  kimberly harvey i'm a dance artist  choreographer  teacher and so i have my own company  subtle craft car  and then i'm a freelance artist and i  also  work for kanduko dance company as a  kentico artist  [Music]  for all of the work that i do whether  that be  choreographer as a teacher one of the  key themes for me is always relationship  whether that be to a space or to other  people or to  myself so that's what runs through  everything and then on top of that  over the past maybe a couple of years  i've been  gathering research around themes of  holding  and both on my own and also with other  artists  um and so this idea of holding spaces in  different ways  and i'm drawn to spaces that hold  me so i will often  choose to put myself in  corners or like behind pillars or in  alcoves  where i kind of um  yeah there's a sense of being held by  the space  i sort of have two works that really  stand out for me one is um a solo that i  made most recently  um called inky mata  and it's basically i'm fascinated by  letters and particularly handwritten  ones  and for ages i thought that it was only  me  um and and and then i sort of realized  that i had lots of  letters in me that i'd never written or  that i had never sent  and then the more i talk to people the  more i realized that  they did as well um  and i also found that for me i  placed the letters in very particular  parts of my body  so that was a as a doctor and maker that  was a  perfect starting point  [Laughter]  and then the other one is a film i made  a few years ago now  and which is called on the tip of my  tongue  which i made with a  friend and colleague of mine and the  reason i love it  is because it came from me  going to andrew graham who's  um the other performer and saying  we've known each other for four years  but i've never actually  directed with you i love what you do as  a dancer and as a  as an artist i want to work with you and  i want to make a dance film  and and he said and i said to him would  you be up for it and he said yes  um and from there we then  sort of created the brief of the film  together  and it was what is our language as a  duet  and because he's almost six foot  and i'm definitely not six foot  um and i'm yes i'm out of the chair for  part of it as well  and so we did some in the chair and some  out of the chair and  because of the relationship that we have  it's also one of trust so i was able  to do things with him in a very short  space of time  [Music]  i love that my job  enables me to play  and to communicate in a variety of ways  as a teacher as a choreographer and as a  performer  and i think as adults particularly we  leave  the ability to play we get told that  we're too old to play or that  you know we should be serious now  because we're adults the the dancing  in a dance environment you see a micro  world which is  really beautiful and things happen in in  a making in a  creative setting that you kind of wish  you could  diffuse to everybody because we're not  frightened of proximity we're  less fearful of touch and we're less  fearful of communicating in a variety of  diverse ways there's an openness in a  creative space which there isn't  to the wider world  [Applause]  you

